{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261113T181838Z-6a5fe582", "ts": "2026-11-13T18:24:11Z", "actor": {"worktree": "arrt-player", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/hdmi-brightness", "build": "1151816623d4"}, "body": {"base_commit": "ca343a5ae78d2ca9914a7c3b8b46435615df6ece", "base_tree": "e2cad245851a4d373a32d73218c3b9320b0f1750", "head_tree": "61304f5a82aa2515be9a040ffdf7480127380ff7", "head_commit": "70d6b6c8fe101ae2c3c604adc2aaf43f825b04e1", "dispatch_commit": "70d6b6c8fe101ae2c3c604adc2aaf43f825b04e1", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "standard", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": [".env.example", "deploy/README.md", "deploy/display.service", "deploy/modules-load.d/arrt-player.conf", "src/arrt_player/__main__.py", "src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/ddc.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py", "tests/fakes.py", "tests/test_config.py", "tests/test_ddc.py", "tests/test_deploy_unit.py", "tests/test_screen_brightness.py"], "files_changed": [".env.example", ".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-hdmi-brightness.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/hdmi-output-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", "deploy/README.md", "deploy/display.service", "deploy/modules-load.d/arrt-player.conf", "src/arrt_player/__main__.py", "src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/ddc.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py", "tests/fakes.py", "tests/test_config.py", "tests/test_ddc.py", "tests/test_deploy_unit.py", "tests/test_screen_brightness.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-hdmi-brightness.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/hdmi-output-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The sun computation can stop an HDMI screen wall: astral's ValueError escapes ScreenBrightness outside its try", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `_follow_the_sun` calls `brightness_module.sun_state` before its `try`, and `sun_state` calls astral's `sun(..., dawn_dusk_depression=12)`, which raises `ValueError(\"Sun never reaches 12 degrees below the horizon, at this location.\")` (astral 3.2, `sun.py` line ~751) wherever the sun's lowest point is above -12 degrees. That is every latitude above about 54.6 degrees (90 - 23.44 - 12) for the weeks around the June solstice, and polar latitudes for longer. The ValueError leaves `ScreenDisplay.after`/`idle`, `unavailable` is empty, so `Wall.run` logs `screen.crashed` and re-raises; the restarted wall has `_at=None`, so it is due on its first pass and crashes again. Before this change a screen wall never consulted the sun, so a location set for the Frame (or for the screens) now takes every HDMI screen off the wall for weeks a year at those latitudes. The module docstring and the plan's `Catch specific exceptions` disposition both promise brightness never stops the wall, and `test_a_brightness_fault_never_stops_the_wall` covers only an OSError (learnings: 'When a comment names a failure as unacceptable or a drift it prevents, write the test from that comment, not the diff'). Members: both `sun_state` callers, this one and `displays/frame.py` `_apply_brightness`. Fix it in one place: `brightness.sun_state` (or `_twilight_fraction`) gives a defined answer when the day has no nautical dawn or dusk, rather than each caller catching it. Pin it with a wall-level test at, for example, 60 N on 21 June that asserts the wall keeps showing and the brightness is still written.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Two of the three exceptions ScreenBrightness catches as host faults are untested, and neither is an OSError", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the error-path tests drive only `FakeMonitor.fails` (a PermissionError and an OSError), so `Garbled` and `NoBus` in `except (Garbled, NoBus, OSError)` are undefended. Neither subclasses OSError, so narrowing the tuple to `(OSError,)` leaves the suite green, and then a connector with no `ddc` link (`bus_for` raises `NoBus` from the bus factory) or a cable that drops bytes on every try (`Ddc.read` raises `Garbled`) crashes the wall on every pass. Add one case per member through the wall: a bus factory that raises `NoBus`, and a read that raises `Garbled`. Each should assert one `screen.brightness_failed`, the wall still showing, and recovery once the fault clears. Watch each fail against the narrowed tuple (learnings: 'When a mutation survives, find why: unreachable ..., inert or rescued by a sibling guard ..., or undefended (test it)').", "files": ["tests/test_screen_brightness.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "run_screen_wall's hand-off of the brightness to the screen wall has no test", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: each half of the wiring is tested on its own, and the join between them is not. `test_the_supervisor_hands_its_screen_brightness_to_a_screen_wall` monkeypatches `run_screen_wall` away, the wall tests call `screen_wall` directly, and the entry-point tests call `screen_brightness` directly. So deleting `brightness=screen_brightness(wall, output, brightness, clock)` from `run_screen_wall` (`screen_wall` defaults `brightness=None`) turns the feature off in production and leaves every test green. The Pi acceptance observed it working at f73f547, but nothing keeps it working (learnings: 'When a behaviour matters because something calls or reads it, test through that caller with a non-default value and mutate the lines you changed'). Test `run_screen_wall` itself: patch `entry.screen_ddc` to return a `FakeMonitor`, use settings with non-default levels, run one pass, and assert the monitor was written at the curve's value.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/__main__.py", "tests/test_screen_brightness.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "'On by default' has no off: with a location set, the screens' brightness cannot be turned off", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Why it broke: the owner's requirement in the plan's Goals reads 'On by default for a screen that answers', and the code has no off state other than leaving the location unset. The Frame requires a location (`_frame` requires LATITUDE, LONGITUDE and LOCATION_NAME with TV_ADDRESS), so a host with a Frame and an HDMI monitor cannot keep the Frame on the sun and leave the monitor alone. The README's way back (SCREEN_MIN = SCREEN_MAX) still writes on every start and every replug. Either add the switch the words imply (a key that turns the screens off, said in the start line), or record in the plan's Goals that 'by default' means 'with no setting' and that there is no off, so the reading is the owner's and not the builder's.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Location and the sun-following policy now exist twice, once for the Frame and once for the screens, and the two copies already disagree", "recommendation": "Scope: class, bounded to two pairs of sites. It broke because this diff pulled the location and the sun-following out as a shared concept for the screens and left the Frame on its own copy. (1) Config: `_frame` still parses LATITUDE/LONGITUDE/LOCATION_NAME/LOCATION_REGION into four loose `FrameSettings` fields, and the new `_location` parses the same keys into the new `Location` type. Its docstring says \"the Frame's brightness and a screen's both follow it\", but nothing on the Frame side uses it. The two already differ on the key they share: `_frame` reads BRIGHTNESS_INTERVAL_SECONDS with `_float`, and `_screen_brightness` reads it with `_positive_float`. Because `_screen_brightness` runs on every load, a Frame-only client with BRIGHTNESS_INTERVAL_SECONDS=0 or a negative value, which loaded before this branch, now fails to start, and the error does not mention screens. Any later rule for these keys (a latitude range, say) will land in one copy and not the other. (2) Runtime: `ScreenBrightness._due` plus `_follow_the_sun` repeat `FrameDisplay._apply_brightness` almost line for line: the same interval gate on `clock.monotonic`, the same `sun_state` call, the same `television_brightness` mapping, and the same write-only-on-change rule. That is the owner's \"a hand change sticks\" policy, and with two copies a change to it (#35 dark hours is next) has to be made twice. Fix it by construction: give `FrameSettings` a `Location` built by `_location` and the one interval reader, and move the gate-and-target step (interval, sun, mapped value, or None when not due) into one helper that both drivers call. Leave each driver's write-and-report code where it is.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A failed DDC bus close is logged at DEBUG, which the journal never emits (the closed tv.close_failed defect, recurring)", "recommendation": "ScreenBrightness._drop_the_bus logs `screen.bus_close_failed` with log.debug, the only DEBUG call in src/arrt_player. `__main__.main` configures the root at INFO, and observability-strategy.md's level table says DEBUG is 'Not used ... a DEBUG line would never be emitted'. Its open-items list (item 2, closed 2026-10-27) records this exact defect for tv.close_failed. So a close that fails, the only sign of a descriptor the Player could not release on the I2C device, never reaches the journal. The event also carries no `output` or `wall_id`, and it is missing from the event inventory table. Two fixes work. Log it at WARNING with `output` and `wall_id` and add its row, which keeps the level table's 'DEBUG: not used' true. Or delete the log call if a failed close is inert. Scope: class. The guard that closed item 2 (test_samsung_client.py:test_a_close_that_fails_reaches_the_journal) covers one event only. Per 'When code is added, moved or retired, ask which guards were scoped to the old shape', the class closes only with a check over the whole source tree. The artifact already describes the ast walk of every log call: making that walk a test that rejects any DEBUG-level call would do it.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "hdmi-output-findings.md still says nothing in the Player drives DDC/CI brightness, in two places, in the same bundle that ships it", "recommendation": "Records Pass, bar 'misleads into action'. The \u00a7 What the Pi does table's Brightness row says 'Nothing in the Player does this, and nothing asks it to'. The intro of \u00a7 Controlling the monitor over the cable, added in this bundle, says 'Nothing in the Player does any of this, and nothing asks it to; it is the evidence a requirement would be written from'. Since #34, the Player sets brightness over /dev/i2c-* from Python on every screen that answers, and the same section's own acceptance row records it doing so. An operator reading this hardware record would wrongly expect a brightness they set with the monitor's buttons to stay put: it holds only until the curve moves. An agent planning #35 (dark hours, which the plan says relies on brightness writes never waking a screen) would design against a Player it believes never touches DDC/CI. Scope: instance. Make both sentences relational (brightness is driven by displays/screen_brightness.py, #34; the rest of the feature set is not used). Per 'When you retire, amend, descope or close anything, first grep the whole repo (no --include) for its nouns and OLD wording', grep for 'Nothing in the Player' and 'ddcutil' for other survivors.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/hdmi-output-findings.md"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The slideshow wiring obligation lives only in this plan and a handoff note, and neither survives the merge to reach the slideshow branch", "recommendation": "The plan's Goals record that slideshow mode draws through mpv, not ScreenDisplay, and that 'whichever of the two branches lands second wires brightness into the slideshow's screen as well. That is recorded here so the merge does not drop it.' The only other record is .prawduct/.handoff-notes.md, which is not tracked. feature/slideshow's build-plan-slideshow.md never mentions brightness, and nothing in this bundle's code or tests would fail if a slideshow screen were built without a ScreenBrightness. If this branch lands first, its plan is archived with the scope, and the slideshow builder merges second with nothing in their path that names the obligation. Their screens then silently ignore the owner's 'every HDMI screen that answers DDC/CI' goal. Record it where the second merge will meet it: in the slideshow plan, or better as a construction, such as a test asserting that every screen display path the supervisor builds carries the screen brightness settings. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-hdmi-brightness.md", "src/arrt_player/__main__.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Four goals ship still marked Inferred at the merge boundary, one of which narrows the owner's 'on by default'", "recommendation": "The plan's Goals still list four builder inferences 'flagged in #34 for the owner to challenge', and nothing records a ruling, although the owner was at the monitor for the chunk 03 acceptance. (1) Location is needed: with no LATITUDE/LONGITUDE/LOCATION_NAME, every screen is left alone. The owner asked for brightness 'on by default for a screen that answers', and the code makes it on only with a location (__main__.screen_brightness returns None, config._location). (2) The levels are in the monitor's units and clamped to its reported maximum. (3) No write while the screen is dark or absent. (4) The slideshow wiring, covered in its own finding. Ask the owner to confirm or overrule (1) to (3), and record the answer in the plan's Goals before merge, so the shipped behaviour rests on a ruling rather than an unanswered flag.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-hdmi-brightness.md", "src/arrt_player/config.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "blocking", "title": "record-lint chunk-ref-missing: chunk 02:64", "recommendation": "declared deliverable `displays/screen.py` file does not exist", "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-10"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 9, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The recorded test evidence predates chunk 03's deploy tests", "recommendation": "test-status exits 0 ('session-fresh, not tree-vouched'), so this is not a finding. But the evidence tree 37f381e holds the old `tests/test_deploy_unit.py` (blob e3d8000, HEAD's is 3bb05fb) and no `deploy/modules-load.d/arrt-player.conf`. So `test_the_player_can_reach_a_screens_brightness` and `test_the_bus_the_brightness_needs_is_loaded_at_boot` have no recorded run against this tree. Run the suite once before the PR.", "files": ["tests/test_deploy_unit.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-artifact reconciliation: all six dispositions read true; one rests on a predicate that cannot see the monitor's input", "recommendation": "The six dispositions were checked against the diff. Interface: true; `I2cTransport` is never opened by the suite (test_ddc says so, and no test opens /dev/i2c-*). Specific exceptions: true for bus failures; the sun-computation gap is the finding above. Event fields: true for all four events and `screen.bus_close_failed`. No hardcoded deployment values: true. Server not required: true. The television: 'inapplicable' is defensible, because the norm's verbs are selecting and pressing power, and a brightness write is neither. But its reason ('no television state') leans on `showing`, which is the Player's last draw plus the connector being connected, not what the monitor is displaying. The measured LG has four inputs (HDMI-1/2, DP-1/2), so a monitor switched to a computer still gets sun writes at dusk. If the owner wants that ruled, the question is whether 'whoever is using it' reaches a desk monitor's other input.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/displays/screen.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "A hand-set brightness lasts only as long as the process and the hotplug line", "recommendation": "`_written` starts as None, so every Player restart, every supervisor restart of a crashed wall, and every hotplug drop (`_forget_the_screen`) writes the curve's value over a hand setting even though the curve did not move. That is consistent with the recorded 'asked afresh when it comes back' decision and with the Frame, but the owner's words, 'sticks until the curve's computed value next changes', do not carry that limit. Record the limit beside the requirement. No code change is implied.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Location and the brightness interval are now validated on every host, Frame or not", "recommendation": "`_screen_brightness` runs on every load. A Frame-less host whose .env carries a stray LATITUDE without LOCATION_NAME now refuses to start, where before none of those keys were read. `BRIGHTNESS_INTERVAL_SECONDS` now goes through `_positive_float` for everyone, so a Frame host with 0 (which `_frame`'s `_float` accepts) refuses too. Both are deliberate per data-model.md, but the deploy README's #34 section does not tell an upgrading operator that a half-filled location now stops the Player.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "deploy/README.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The new DDC module has its own copy of how an output name maps to a connector directory", "recommendation": "`ddc._CONNECTOR_DIR` is a character-for-character copy of `client._HDMI_CONNECTOR`, and `bus_for`'s docstring says the match is made \"as `client.hdmi_outputs` matches it\". That is a promise of parity that nothing checks. `kms.KmsOutput` resolves the same name a third way, with a glob. `ddc` also imports `client` only to get `DRM_ROOT`. If the output naming in `client` changes, `bus_for` would raise `NoBus` while the screen keeps drawing, and the only sign would be `screen.brightness_failed`. Cheap fix: one `connector_dirs(output, drm_root)` in `client` that `kms` and `ddc` both call. Scope: class, three sites.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/ddc.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "'Off' is encoded two ways, and ScreenBrightness gets the location twice", "recommendation": "`ClientSettings.screen_brightness` is always present and means off through `location is None`, while `run_screen_wall` also accepts `brightness=None` and `screen_brightness()` checks both. `ScreenBrightness` then takes `settings` (which holds an optional location) and a separate `location=settings.location`, only to narrow the type. The plan said the settings are None when no location is set. Either return None from `_screen_brightness` when there is no location and make `location` required on `ScreenBrightnessSettings`, or drop the separate parameter. This keeps one place that says whether brightness is on. Scope: instance.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/__main__.py", "src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Two docstrings still describe the shape from before this change", "recommendation": "Prose, so no edit is required. The `_frame` docstring says \"The sun drives the television's brightness and nothing else, so a client with no Frame has no use for a location\", which the `_location` code 20 lines below contradicts: delete that clause. The `ddc.py` module docstring says \"`displays/screen.py` decides what to set and when\", but that is `displays/screen_brightness.py`: make it relational (\"its caller decides\") or name the right module. Scope: instance.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/ddc.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The README's alternative way back does not turn screen brightness off", "recommendation": "`deploy/README.md` \u00a7 An HDMI screen's brightness follows the sun gives a second way back: set SCREEN_MAX_BRIGHTNESS and SCREEN_MIN_BRIGHTNESS to the same level, \"which holds the screen there\". The Player still writes that level at start and after every replug, and because only changes are written, a hand change is not held. A Frame client must set a location, so it has no way to leave a screen's brightness alone short of the previous revision. Either say exactly what equal levels do (one write per start or replug, after which hand changes stick), or, if an operator may need DDC writes stopped, record that gap. Scope: instance.", "files": ["deploy/README.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A write whose read-back fails is logged as 'could not set', and its value is never logged as set", "recommendation": "ScreenBrightness._set assigns self._written before the read-back. If that read raises (NoAnswer, Garbled, OSError), _follow_the_sun logs screen.brightness_failed ('could not set the brightness'), even though the write went out. On the next interval value == _written, so _set returns None: the journal says 'can be set again' and never carries screen.brightness_set for the value on the wall until the curve moves. deploy/README.md tells the operator to look for that event. This is rare and costs at most one curve step of journal accuracy. Logging the set before the read-back, or treating a failed read-back as read_back=None on the set event, would close it.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/displays/screen_brightness.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "observability-strategy.md's inventory counts are now short", "recommendation": "'114 distinct names', '109 call sites' and '16 modules' predate the five new log calls in displays/screen_brightness.py, a new module with a logger. Nothing reads these figures, and the artifact itself says no test enforces the walk. No edit is wanted beyond whatever re-derivation the DEBUG finding's fix prompts.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "data-model.md's Frame row says the location keys and BRIGHTNESS_INTERVAL_SECONDS are read 'only when [TV_ADDRESS] is set'", "recommendation": "Those keys are now read on every Player, for screen brightness. A half-set location with no TV_ADDRESS, which was ignored before, now stops the start with a ConfigError. The new Screen brightness row directly below says they are shared, and the ConfigError names the missing key, so a reader is corrected at once. This clears no Records Pass bar.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "scope-trace: I2cTransport.device has no consumer", "recommendation": "The public `device` property on ddc.I2cTransport is read by nothing in src/ or tests/. It is a one-line property, so delete it or leave it.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/ddc